 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "eq:2.2.4a",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "vibrating-string",
   "prompt": "<p>Waves travel along a string at $\\kv = 2.0$ m/s. Its wave equation is separated with $\\ku(\\kx,\\kt) = X(\\kx)T(\\kt)$, and the separation constant is $K = -4.0\\ \\text{m}^{-2}$. Write the two ordinary differential equations, and find the $\\komega$ for which the time equation reads $\\dfrac{d^2T}{d\\kt^2} + \\komega^2 T = 0$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "value": 4.0,
    "unit": "rad/s",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "In Equation 2.2.7, $-K\\kv^2$ stands where $\\komega^2$ does.",
    "solution": "<p>The separation gives $\\dfrac{d^2T}{d\\kt^2} - K\\kv^2T = 0$ and $\\dfrac{d^2X}{d\\kx^2} - KX = 0$ (Equations 2.2.7 and 2.2.8). With $K = -4.0\\ \\text{m}^{-2}$ and $\\kv = 2.0$ m/s, $-K\\kv^2 = (4.0\\ \\text{m}^{-2})(2.0\\ \\text{m/s})^2 = 16\\ \\text{s}^{-2}$, so the time equation is $\\dfrac{d^2T}{d\\kt^2} + (16\\ \\text{s}^{-2})\\,T = 0$ and $\\komega = 4.0$ rad/s. The space equation is $\\dfrac{d^2X}{d\\kx^2} + (4.0\\ \\text{m}^{-2})\\,X = 0$.</p>"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "source_id": "eq:2.2.7b",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Analyze",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "vibrating-string",
   "prompt": "<p>A string is held at zero at $\\kx = 0$ and $\\kx = \\kL$. Take a separation constant $K > 0$ and show that the general solution $X(\\kx) = Ae^{\\sqrt{K}\\kx} + Be^{-\\sqrt{K}\\kx}$ meets both boundary conditions only when $A = B = 0$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Use the condition at $\\kx = 0$ to write $B$ in terms of $A$.",
    "solution": "<p>The condition at $\\kx = 0$ gives $A + B = 0$, so $B = -A$ (Equation 2.2.12). The condition at $\\kx = \\kL$ then reads $A\\left(e^{\\sqrt{K}\\kL} - e^{-\\sqrt{K}\\kL}\\right) = 0$ (Equation 2.2.13). With $K > 0$ and $\\kL > 0$, $\\sqrt{K}\\kL > 0$, so $e^{\\sqrt{K}\\kL} > 1 > e^{-\\sqrt{K}\\kL}$ and the bracket is not zero. Hence $A = 0$, and then $B = -A = 0$. Only the trivial solution $X(\\kx) = 0$ is left, so a string held at both ends carries no wave with $K > 0$.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
